{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift to the whole path when recent returns are negative.",
    "Trend-lag / insufficient bearish momentum: after a local peak, the model declines too slowly and keeps predicting rebounds that do not materialize. Increase the magnitude of negative drift when the last 10–15 days show a clear lower-high/lower-low pattern.",
    "Overshoot on rebounds: the model projects sharp recoveries (late-horizon ramps into mid/high-80s) while outcomes stay flat-to-lower (high-60s/low-70s). Cap upside slope and enforce mean-reversion toward recent realized levels rather than extrapolating model-internal rallies.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: forecast error grows with horizon; near-term is mildly high, weeks 2–4 are strongly high. Use a horizon-weighted correction that increases the downward adjustment with lead time (e.g., small cut days 1–5, larger cut days 10–30).",
    "Volatility miscalibration: forecast path is too smooth and centered too high; realized series shows deeper drawdowns. When recent realized volatility rises, widen downside risk and shift the distribution lower (do not compensate with symmetric upside).",
    "Regime switch heuristic: if spot is below its 20–30d moving average and the MA slope is negative, force forecasts to stay below the MA (no crossing back above) unless a strong positive shock appears in the last 3–5 days."
  ]
}
